{title:'The repair café',tag:'Everyday life · Article',text:`On the first Sunday of every month, a room in Westbridge Community Centre becomes a repair café. Visitors bring broken lamps, torn jackets and small household objects. Volunteers try to repair them while their owners watch and, when possible, help. There is no fixed charge, although donations pay for basic materials.

The café was started by a retired engineer, Daniel Price. He noticed that neighbours were throwing away objects with minor problems. At first, he thought the main benefit would be less waste. After several months, however, he realised that visitors valued the conversations just as much as the repairs. Some older residents began coming even when they had nothing broken to bring.

Not every object can be repaired. Volunteers refuse items that could be unsafe, and sometimes a replacement part costs more than the object itself. The organisers therefore ask visitors not to expect a guaranteed repair. They also explain that the café is not a free professional repair shop: learning together matters more than finishing quickly.

Last month, a teenager named Ella brought a jacket with a broken zip. Instead of fixing it for her, a volunteer showed her how to replace the zip herself. It took most of the afternoon. Ella said she could have bought a cheap new jacket in less time, but now she had a skill she could use again. Daniel sees experiences like hers as evidence that the café is working. He hopes to recruit more volunteers, especially people who can repair bicycles, but he does not plan to make the café a weekly event yet.`,q:[
['How often does the café take place?',['Every Sunday','Once a month','Twice a week','Every afternoon'],1,'Встреча проходит в первое воскресенье каждого месяца.'],
['What originally motivated Daniel?',['Selling replacement parts','Teaching teenagers only','Creating a paid repair business','Reducing unnecessary waste'],3,'Сначала главной пользой он считал уменьшение отходов.'],
['What surprised Daniel about visitors?',['They also valued social contact','They preferred new objects','They refused to watch repairs','They all wanted to volunteer'],0,'Посетители ценили разговоры не меньше, чем ремонт.'],
['Why might volunteers refuse an item?',['Its owner is too young','It was bought recently','It could be dangerous','It is not expensive'],2,'В тексте прямо указано: небезопасные вещи не принимают в ремонт.'],
['What did Ella gain from the visit?',['A free new jacket','An ability she could use in the future','A job at the centre','A faster way to shop'],1,'Элла научилась менять молнию — этот навык пригодится снова.'],
['Which statement matches Daniel’s current plans?',['Open every week immediately','Stop accepting clothing','Charge every visitor','Find volunteers with bicycle repair skills'],3,'Он ищет дополнительных волонтёров, особенно умеющих чинить велосипеды.'] ]},
{title:'When a shortcut is not faster',tag:'Ideas · Science & society',text:`A city transport team recently tested a simple idea: could better information encourage more people to walk? Many residents drove between the central station and a shopping street because they believed the walk took at least twenty minutes. The actual journey on foot was usually eight minutes. Driving could take longer once finding a parking space was included.

The team placed signs at important junctions. Instead of showing only distances, each sign gave the approximate walking time to familiar places. The signs also pointed out routes through public gardens. During a six-week trial, the number of people walking from the station to the shopping street increased. Interviews suggested that the time estimates helped people reconsider their usual choices.

However, the researchers were careful about their conclusions. The trial took place in spring, when the weather was unusually pleasant. A new café also opened near the walking route during the same period. Either change might have encouraged walking independently of the signs. To understand the signs' effect more clearly, the team plans to compare similar areas with and without them during the same months.

The project also revealed a limitation of simple time estimates. A route described as an eight-minute walk may take considerably longer for someone with limited mobility. After receiving feedback, the team added information about steps and steep slopes to its online map. The researchers argue that useful travel information should not tell everyone what to do. Instead, it should help different people make decisions that suit their circumstances.`,q:[
['Why did many residents choose to drive?',['Walking was forbidden','They overestimated the walking time','The station had moved','There were no public gardens'],1,'Жители думали, что идти не меньше 20 минут, хотя обычно путь занимал 8.'],
['What was distinctive about the signs?',['They advertised parking','They showed only distances','They gave estimated walking times','They recommended one café'],2,'На указателях было примерное время пешего пути.'],
['What happened during the trial?',['More people walked along the studied route','All residents stopped driving','Parking became free','The shopping street closed'],0,'Количество пешеходов на изучаемом маршруте выросло.'],
['Why were the researchers cautious?',['Nobody read the signs','They had lost their results','The trial lasted several years','Other changes could have affected walking'],3,'Хорошая погода и новое кафе могли повлиять на результат отдельно от указателей.'],
['What does “them” in “with and without them” refer to?',['Researchers','Signs','Gardens','Interviews'],1,'Планируется сравнить похожие районы с указателями и без них.'],
['What is the author’s main conclusion?',['Everyone should walk at the same speed','Time estimates should replace all maps','Information should support people with different needs','Driving is always slower than walking'],2,'Информация должна помогать разным людям выбирать с учётом своих обстоятельств.'] ]}
