{title:'A quieter experiment',tag:'Ideas · Research',text:`At Brookside School, a teacher noticed that students disagreed about background music during independent work. Some said it helped them concentrate; others found it distracting. Rather than hold a vote, she invited the class to investigate the question during four short reading sessions.

Two sessions took place with quiet instrumental music and two without music. Students answered questions about each text and rated how focused they felt. The teacher alternated the conditions instead of placing all the silent sessions first. She also tried to choose texts of similar length and difficulty.

The results were mixed. Students often reported enjoying the music, but enjoyment did not always correspond to more correct answers. Several students performed better in silence, while others showed little difference. One student pointed out that being unfamiliar with a text's topic could affect scores as much as the room's sound.

The teacher did not claim that the activity proved which environment was best for everyone. The class was small, and four sessions could not represent every kind of schoolwork. Instead, they agreed to keep shared reading periods quiet and allow headphones during certain individual creative tasks. The experiment helped them discuss their needs using observations rather than assuming that everyone worked in the same way.`,q:[
['Why did the teacher organise the activity?',['To choose a school song','To investigate different views about music','To replace reading lessons','To select the best musician'],1,'Ученики по-разному оценивали влияние музыки, и учитель предложила это изучить.'],
['What did students record besides answers?',['Their feelings about concentration','Their favourite instruments','Their journey times','Their homework schedules'],0,'Они оценивали, насколько сосредоточенными себя чувствовали.'],
['What did the results suggest?',['Music helped every student','Enjoyment guaranteed accuracy','Silence prevented learning','Enjoying music did not necessarily improve scores'],3,'Музыка могла нравиться, но это не всегда означало больше правильных ответов.'],
['What other influence did a student mention?',['Classroom temperature','The price of headphones','Familiarity with the topic','The length of the school year'],2,'Знакомство с темой текста тоже могло влиять на результат.'],
['Why were the conclusions limited?',['No one completed the tasks','The activity involved few students and sessions','The music was always loud','Only creative tasks were tested'],1,'Небольшой класс и четыре занятия не позволяют делать выводы обо всей учёбе.'],
['What was agreed for shared reading?',['It would take place in silence','Everyone would wear headphones','Students would choose songs together','It would be replaced by drawing'],0,'Общие периоды чтения решили проводить в тишине.'] ]}
